A||||7||Sevaan||Broken Down! Need Help!||||4||
Z||000000||Sevaan||03-11-2001||11:38 AM||||Hey guys,<P>So I drove to work yesterday without any problems. Afterwards I was ready to come home. I got in the car and put the key in the ignition and turned. Nothing. Not a sound. My car won't start and it's still sitting in that parking lot all alone.<P>The battery is not the problem. It's charged up and all the lights work, radio, etc. I tested those all out. I think the problem is something to do with the shift linkage or the Neutral start switch.<P>It's happen before, but all I did was roughly shift through all the gears (my car is an automatic), and it started up. Not this time though.<P>I fiddled with the car for two hours last night but nothing came of it. And just this morning I went back to try again in the light, but I still can't get anything out of it.<P>So I'm back home now, but I brought the neutral start switch with me to clean up (But I doubt that it's a problem).<P>Any help at all would be appreciated! I'm missing my wheels already!||||reg||4
Z||000001||Indyellowgt||03-11-2001||12:27 PM||||Maybe your starter silinoid(spelling?) went out??<BR>I had the exact same prob with an 85se auto 4cyl.<BR>All power was there,tried the park/neutral switch,then got the guts to climb under there and presto,after I installed the new starter,fired up perfectly.<BR>When you go to start it,just as you turn the key in the final start position(all the way)<BR>leave your decklid open and listen.if you hear a *click* then chances are it is the starter.The silinoid is not engaging the starter drive.Somtimes giving it a good whack(if you can rech it)will snap it out of it to get you started to get home!<BR>Give it a try,it worked for me!!<P>-Mark||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay||03-11-2001||12:29 PM||||Just a thought, this has happened to me, take the cable for the automatic shifter off of the tranny.  Then move the linkage on the tranny all the way up to Park.  On my car the cable was kinking and was not allowing the linkage to move fully into Park.  Sometimes it worked sometimes it didn't, It depended on how the kink was lined up.  Just a thought.  Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||03-11-2001||12:36 PM||||You could go round and round on something like this.  Get your shop manual and start the diagnostic flow chart.  One step at a time will save you time and effort on figuring it out, unless you stumble on it by dumb luck.  One thing I would ask is, if you turn the headlights on, and try to start the car, are the lights dimming?  If not, then your starter circuit is not engaging at all.  That'll at least give you just 1 subsystem to look at.||||reg||3
Z||000004||theogre||03-11-2001||01:22 PM||||Did you do the obvious and try starting in neutral? Did you check to see if the switch was loose on the tranny? <P>If it has tilt wheel did you try diferant positions? sometimes the parts get sloppy in one spot.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sevaan||03-11-2001||07:52 PM||||Thanks for the help guys! Expecially Indyellowgt, because it was the solenoid. It was bitter cold and my digits were falling off as I fiddled with the neutral switch, but after reinstalling it I had no luck. So I gave the solenoid a whack with my hammer and it worked! It got me home in one piece, except now I can't start it again.<P>Is there any way to servie the solenoid myself? And if not, how much does a new one cost (Approx)?<P>Thanks again for all your help guys. You really saved my butt!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||03-11-2001||07:58 PM||||Just for grins, check the connections where the wires connect to the starter. I've seen loose connections there cause a problem. (Not on a Fiero, but still...)<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sevaan||03-14-2001||10:56 PM||||Raydar was right. A wire had frayed right through and was just dangling off, so I re-stripped it and made a new connection for the wires. The car starts up beautifully now.<P>It was a pain to do though reaching down from the top of the car. Took me hours to do that simple job, only because I lost a nut and then tried to stick another one on only to find that it was too small all this time! Finally I got it on, but realized that the connection I had made was too thick, so I had to change that too. It was a long and arduous experience, but it's finally done now. Whew.||||reg||1
